// The `gir:` import scheme: a GObject library's widgets, declared from its
// introspection data.
//
// `import gtk "gir:Gtk-4.0"` is a package holding one component per class
// the GIR describes -- named by its C type, `gtk.GtkLabel` -- with a prop
// per writable property, an event per signal a handler can be connected to,
// and a method per action: a GIR method returning nothing whose values are
// each a `string`, `bool`, `int` or `float`, and which no property names as
// its setter -- `bar.pulse()`, `label.setMarkup("<b>x</b>")`. A string GIR
// says the method takes (transfer-ownership="full") is `#[macro.moves]`, so
// the program does not free it. Each is an `#[intrinsic]` primitive the gtk4
// platform renders, so a program naming one builds for gtk4 alone.
// sngl:platform/gtk4's own wrappers are written against it.
//
// Which introspection data is read is gtk4's `gir` option, from the command
// line or the program's `output` block: unset, the system locations are
// probed and the bundled subset is the fallback; `builtin` is the bundled
// subset; anything else is a path. What the import wrote is
// stored, and written again when the option or the file it names changes.
